                        "review": "\nToki Tori made me believe in the video games industry as an institution again, this game is a priceless example of consistent through-put in a work environment, Two Tribes has managed to release stellar Toki Tori games all through out the decade. While, yes, Toki Tori is a masterpiece, it is also the reason why the video game industry has not yet collapsed.\n\nToki Tori is a puzzle platformer game about a young chick named Toki Tori, a delightfully youthful free-spirited protagonist, on a quest to rescue eggs.\nNow thats just a mere plot synopsis, but there is more in Toki Tori than what meets the eye. Truth is, Toki Tori is a game about perseverance and familial bonds.\nWhen Toki Tori falls into a hole and gets sad, you truly feel like you commited a sin you cannot wash away,\nwhen you spin Toki Tori around to make him busy, you feel like you're playing with one of your siblings, like you yourself exist in the game world through one of the eggs he collects, you are the egg, engaging your older brother in a good bit of horse play and the like.\nToki Tori is a game about using your brain, because Toki Tori loves you and wants the best for you, and in the same vein, you want the best for Toki Tori,\nI shed several real tears after one too many of my mistakes caused Toki Tori's untimely demise several times over, \nthis is because the game immerses you so deep in its simple puzzle solving world, you really feel like his blood is on your very own hands. \nToki Tori is happy when you succeed, and when you fail, it tears you apart inside. I've spent many sleepless nights staring up at my ceiling beating myself up over all those times I let Toki Tori down,\nbut then I remember, practice is what makes us human, and Toki Tori wouldn't want me to feel bad about myself. The thought of Toki Tori caring about me, smiling at me and believing I'm able to do great things makes me \ndrift off to sleep peacefully and washes all those bad thoughts away.\nEssentially, Toki Tori has become part of my daily routine, while yes it sounds hyperbolic, this games ability to affect your thoughts and feelings will change you as a person.\nBefore discovering Toki Tori I felt like a truly broken man, but those days are behind me, this is due to the game's incredible puzzle design.\n\nIn a clever twist on the video game platformer genre, Toki Tori actually doesn't posses the ability to jump, not ever since the accident.\nThis leads to lots of tense brilliant gameplay situations where Toki Tori is forced to navigate the in-game world in new unexpected ways.\nNow while Toki Tori's inability to jump would already make for a great subversive game, the developers went above and beyond by introducing tools.\nGadgets such as platforms, guns, magic and more. I was admittedly skeptical at first that Toki Tori wouldn't be able to handle an intricate item system at first while already being beyond ambitious in every single other conceiveable way, \nbut the developers proved me wrong. Each tool blends seamlessly into the puzzle design, creating one of a kind emergant gameplay dependent on the players wit and creativity alone.\nThis game is a tool for funneling out joy and raw emotion out of you disguised as a simple puzzle platformer video game. \n\nThe developers care about nothing more than seeing happiness throughout the world increase, seeing as they basically give this game out for FREE while managing \nto stay afloat and keep on creating great things. I commend you, Two Tribes.\n\nI think many of gamings greatest hits have taken cues from Toki Tori, have you ever considered Mario Bros? An unpopular choice for sure, \nbut the resemblence is uncanny. \nIn Super Mario Brothers Wii, a new power-up is introduced, the cunning \"Ice Flower\" that turns enemies into solid blocks of ice. Hmm, haven't we heard that one before?\nYes, that's right. Super Mario Brothers Wii basically copied Toki Tori's ice gun! And believe it or not, the examples run much deeper than that. \nPostal 2 is another famous gaming classics gamers will be no doubt familiar with, but did you know Toki Tori inspiration runs deep throughout it's franchise as well? \nToki Tori has many iconic pop-culture references hidden throughout it's steam achievements, like a clever nod to the Reddit meme sensation of \"Rick Astley's\" Never Gonna Give You Up.\nWouldn't you know it, Postal 2 itself is also chalk full of clever nods to pop-culture sprinkled throughout it's steam achievements, such as the reference to \"Modern Family\".\nYes, it's true Toki Tori inspirations runs all throughout the game industry's cold shoulders, and it's why I believe gaming to still not be dead, because as long\nas Toki Tori's blood is in circulation, developers will keep on making great things.\n\nWith all said and done, words cannot describe the level of excellence Toki Tori exudes, inspiring gamings greatest hits as well as real life emotions, Toki Tori deserves a Noble Peace Prize reward for being \nthe reason why humanity was brought a little closer together, and if all the world leaders gathered in a room and solved Toki Tori puzzles with each other we would achieve world peace by tommorow no joke I actually believe this.\nMy theory is actually corroborated by the fact the original Toki Tori released mere 15 days after the september 11 terrorist attack, in which several planes were hijacked and \nflown into the Twin Towers. I personally theorize that Two Tribes released their game as a call to peace among worlds, attempting to unite people that were affected by the tragedy and prevent further escalation of the conflict,\nand while they might have not succeded just yet, they are sure to succeded several decades from now, as it is my belief Toki Tori is the solution to a utopian society.\nBecause Karl Marx was uneducated and a bit daft, he tried illustrating his ideas of utopian society as a socialist society, \nit is my belief that Marx was actually trying to describe Toki Tori for the Gameboy Color and simply wasn't nearly articulate enough to\nsufficently explain his ahead of time views to the wider public and made up a bunch of nonsense about social reform instead. \nToki Tori",
